--json headRefOid`. If they differ, `git fetch origin
+refs/pull/<n>/head:refs/remotes/origin/pr/<n>` and `git reset --hard
origin/pr/<n>`, but only when `git status --porcelain` is empty and the
checkout is a review workspace (detached, or on the pull request's head
branch): never move another branch to the pull request's head.

```bash
gh pr view <url> --json title,body,author,baseRefName,headRefOid,files,reviews,comments
gh pr diff <url>
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n>/comments --paginate   # inline threads
```

Read the linked issue or card when the body links one. The pull request's
stated goal is what "correct" is measured against. Gather whatever else the
guide asks for before judging.

**This is a later round.** Find your last review in the `reviews` of the `gh pr view` above: your latest entry (`gh api user --jq .login` names you) gives its `commit.oid` and your last verdict.

- Diff only what changed since your last review:
  `git diff <last_reviewed_commit_id>..HEAD`. A force-push can make it unreachable; then
  review the full diff and say so in the body.
- For each of your previous threads, check whether the new code addresses
  it; section 4a says how to answer each one.
- Keep the scope of new findings the guide sets for later rounds.

### 2. Find the issues

Apply the guide. A complete review has independent parts; when you can,
delegate them to parallel subagents and keep for yourself the verification,
the consolidation and the writing. Run each delegate the guide lists, on
this round's diff range.

Each subagent or delegate gets the pull request URL, the diff range of this
round and the part it owns, and returns findings only: `path:line`, what is
wrong, a failing scenario or the rule quoted, and a proposed label.
Subagents read; they never comment, publish or change files. A small diff
can be reviewed without delegating.

Then consolidate: merge duplicates, and verify every finding against the
code yourself before keeping it.

**The minimum for every finding**, whatever the guide says: it points to a
line (or, when it has none, says so), it describes a concrete failing
scenario or quotes the rule it breaks, and it is never invented. A finding
you cannot ground is dropped.

### 3. Classify

Every comment starts with a bold label from the guide. When the guide
defines none, use [Conventional Comments](https://conventionalcomments.org)
